### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence clearly demonstrates that Eric Trump, Donald Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump are not banned from operating charities. While the Donald J. Trump Foundation was dissolved following a lawsuit by the New York Attorney General over the misuse of funds, the resulting 2019 court settlement did not legally bar the Trump children from running charitable organizations. Instead, they were required to complete mandatory training on the fiduciary duties of charity directors. Although there were public allegations and reports that the Eric Trump Foundation diverted donations intended for pediatric cancer patients to Trump-owned businesses and other interests, the claim's central assertion of a lifetime ban is legally incorrect.

### Conclusion
Based on the documented evidence, the claim that Eric Trump, Donald Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump are banned from ever operating a charity again is false. The legal settlement resolving the investigation into their foundation specifically did not include a ban on their future charitable activities, opting instead for compulsory educational training. Therefore, the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The assertion that the Trump children are permanently banned from operating charities is false. According to court records, a New York court did not legally ban Eric, Donald Jr., or Ivanka Trump from running charitable organizations ([Snopes](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-steal-kids-cancer-charity/)). While the Donald J. Trump Foundation was dissolved following allegations of self-dealing, the 2019 settlement only required the three children to undergo mandatory training on the fiduciary duties of charity directors ([Politifact](https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/oct/24/facebook-posts/trump-family-disallowed-operating-charities-new-yo/), [NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/donald-trump/judge-orders-trump-pay-2-million-misusing-his-foundation-n1078306)). Furthermore, although reports revealed that the Eric Trump Foundation diverted some donations intended for pediatric cancer patients to Trump properties and other interests ([ABC News](https://abcnews.com/Politics/eric-trump-funneled-cancer-charity-money-businesses-associates/story?id=47878610)), these actions did not result in any lifetime ban on their charitable activities.